Choosing a channel library does not by itself answer who owns the data, when a receiver may see a write, or who terminates a stalled computation. Concurrency offers several distinct guarantees: a synchronization rule relates actions across threads, an ownership rule limits access to data, and a protocol rule determines which message may arrive next. A design can be strong on one and silent on another. A handoff can also transfer the cleanup obligation discussed in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-183}.

\section{What a Send Actually Promises}

Alan A.~A. Donovan and Brian W. Kernighan's \emph{The Go Programming Language} builds pipelines with goroutines and channels and treats cancellation separately. For a matched send and receive, writes before the send are visible to operations after the corresponding receive under the memory model's happens-before rule. But sending a slice copies its descriptor, not its backing array. The rule orders writes before the send; it does not give the reader exclusive access. If the sender subsequently overwrites that array while the receiver reads it, synchronization of the handoff alone is insufficient.

Steve Klabnik and Carol Nichols's \emph{The Rust Programming Language} distinguishes \texttt{Send}, which permits transferring a value to another thread, from \texttt{Sync}, which permits sharing references between threads (a type \(T\) is \texttt{Sync} if \verb|&T| is \texttt{Send}). Sending an owned byte vector can move ownership and end the sender's ability to use that value until it gets ownership back. By contrast, passing a shared reference to a synchronization primitive permits shared access according to that primitive's API. This is more specific than saying that Rust forbids shared memory, and it makes no claim that a thread will ever obtain a lock.

\section{Worked Example: A Bounded Buffer Pool}

Give a producer \(N\) buffers. It fills a buffer and sends it to a worker. When the worker finishes reading, it returns the buffer for reuse. At any instant each buffer is on the free list, in transit, or privately held by exactly one stage. In Go, sending a slice down the work channel and waiting for its return down the recycling channel can implement this contract. Queue capacity constrains how many sends can complete without corresponding receives; it does not prevent the producer from retaining an alias. Hide access behind an API or copy the contents if the producer cannot honor the transfer rule. In Rust, send owned vectors in both directions. The compiler rejects a subsequent use of the moved vector, rather than expecting a test to reveal that it was reused prematurely.

Now start with \(N=1\), fill the only buffer, and cancel after the work send but before the worker returns it. If the producer exits and the worker blocks indefinitely on an unbuffered return, the ownership invariant may hold while the shutdown test fails. In Go, select between the return send and a cancellation signal, and arrange for all stages to stop or drain as specified. In Rust, endpoint disconnection can be one stop condition: with an unbuffered return channel, dropping its final receiver causes a pending send to fail and return the unsent value. Other outstanding operations still need a cancellation policy. In either implementation assign one actor the responsibility for closing any shared Go channel, or for ensuring all Rust sender clones have been dropped where disconnection signals completion.

\section{Specify Message Order Separately}

Manuel F\"ahndrich and colleagues' \emph{Language Support for Fast and Reliable Message-based Communication in Singularity OS} describes pointer transfers that pass ownership and statically verified channel contracts that constrain valid interactions. A language with such contracts can say that a worker accepts a buffer in state \texttt{Ready}, owes a return in state \texttt{Busy}, and cannot accept another buffer through that endpoint before returning the first. A typed buffer channel alone does not impose that sequence, whether or not the message type is safe to move.

Test the protocol with empty input, a worker error while holding a buffer, cancellation at each handoff, and enough work to fill every queue. Assert conservation of buffer identities until the documented teardown step, and separately assert that all stages reach a terminal state. A data-race detector is useful for the Go implementation, but a clean run does not prove eventual shutdown. See the \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/23-compiler-as-an-actor-system.md}{dossier's Compiler as an Actor System} for a proposed architecture where these coordination choices become compiler infrastructure.
